"""One extra practical section for batch 31 GA4 guides (general advice; Google facts stay on the main pages)."""

X = {
"google-analytics-4-key-events": ("How to name events consistently", "Before creating events, agree on a short list of names and use them everywhere. Google's recommended names, such as generate_lead for a form submission, are the best starting point because reports and future features recognize them. For actions without a recommended name, use short lowercase names with underscores, such as phone_click or directions_click. Use the same name on every page and form, so a quote form on a service page and a contact form on the homepage both send generate_lead rather than two different names. Keep a note of each event, what triggers it and when it was marked as a key event, so anyone reviewing reports later knows what the numbers mean."),
"google-analytics-utm-parameters": ("Where Orange County businesses lose attribution", "The biggest gaps we see come from links nobody thinks of as marketing. The website button on a Google Business Profile is often the busiest source of local visits, yet untagged it blends into organic search. Links in email signatures, text-message reminders, PDF quotes and invoices, printed flyers, vehicle wraps and event banners usually arrive as Direct traffic unless tagged, often through a QR code. Directory listings you control, such as Yelp or industry directories, can carry tagged links too. Go through every place your website address appears, decide which ones you can tag and add them to your naming sheet. Even tagging the five busiest sources makes reports far easier to trust, and it shows which offline marketing in Anaheim and across Orange County actually drives visits. Review the list whenever you print new materials or add a new listing, so new links are tagged from the start."),
"google-analytics-4-traffic-acquisition-report": ("Understanding Direct and Referral traffic", "Two channels often confuse owners. Direct includes people who typed your address or used a bookmark, but also clicks from untagged emails, apps, documents and some messaging apps, so a large Direct share usually means links need tagging rather than that people know your address by heart. Referral covers clicks from other websites, including directories, partners, local news sites and AI assistants such as ChatGPT or Perplexity. Click into Referral to see the individual sites. For local businesses, this is where you discover which directories, community sites and partners actually send visitors, and which listings are worth keeping up to date. Tagging your own links moves them out of Direct and into the right channel, making the rest of the report clearer. Review the referral list every few months to spot new sites linking to you."),
"google-analytics-4-realtime-report": ("Excluding your own visits", "Testing in Realtime means your own visits appear in reports. For a small business with modest traffic, staff and owner visits can noticeably inflate numbers over time. Google Analytics lets administrators define internal traffic, for example by the office's IP address, and filter it out of reports once the filter is active. Set this up after you've finished testing, and keep a test mode available for future checks. Also remember that visits from your web designer or agency during site work count too. A clean property, where tests and staff visits are excluded, gives you numbers that reflect real customers in Anaheim and across Orange County, which is what you need when deciding where to spend time and money. Check the filter after any office move or internet provider change."),
"link-google-ads-to-google-analytics": ("Questions owners ask us", "Owners often ask whether linking shares their data with Google in new ways. Linking allows data to flow between your own Analytics property and Ads account for the purposes Google describes, such as conversions and audiences; review the link settings and your privacy policy to make sure they match how you use the data. Another common question is whether to import key events or use the Google Ads tag for conversions. Importing key events keeps both products consistent and is simpler for most small businesses, as long as you don't count the same action twice. Owners also ask whether a link helps Local Services Ads. It doesn't; Local Services Ads have their own lead reporting. Finally, owners ask how long until bidding improves. Smart bidding needs a steady flow of conversions, so give it several weeks before judging the change. Keep notes of any bidding or budget changes so you can match them to results later, and review lead quality alongside the conversion count."),
"google-analytics-4-data-retention": ("A first-week settings checklist", "Data retention is one of several settings worth checking in the first week of any Analytics property, because some only affect data collected afterwards. Confirm the reporting time zone is Pacific time for an Orange County business and the currency is right. Set data retention to 14 months. Turn on enhanced measurement and test it. Mark your main lead actions as key events. Define internal traffic so staff visits can be filtered. Link Search Console and Google Ads if you use them. Add at least two business-controlled Administrators and give agencies narrower roles. Doing these things on day one costs an hour; discovering a year later that a setting was wrong costs a year of comparable data. Write the settings down in a short document so anyone who works on the property later knows how it was configured and why, and review the list once a year to make sure nothing has drifted, especially after a website redesign or a change of agency."),
"google-analytics-4-users-and-permissions": ("Taking back control of an old account", "Many businesses find that a former designer, agency or employee created their Analytics account and still controls it. If that person still has Administrator access, ask them to add a business-controlled Google Account as an Administrator, then lower or remove their access. If they won't respond, Analytics access depends on the account holder, so you may need to contact them in writing or, as a last resort, create a new property and accept that history stays in the old one. Prevention is far easier: when anyone sets up Analytics for you, make sure the account is created with or immediately shared to a business account you control. The same applies to Search Console, Google Business Profile and Google Ads, so check them all at once."),
"google-analytics-4-enhanced-measurement": ("Which options matter for local businesses", "Not every option is equally useful. Page views are always on and form interactions are the most valuable for lead-based businesses. Outbound clicks matter when bookings, orders or payments happen on another site, such as a scheduling tool or delivery platform. File downloads matter for businesses that share menus, price lists or brochures as PDFs. Site search only helps if your site has a search box. Scrolls help decide where to place calls to action on long service pages. Video engagement matters if you embed YouTube videos on key pages. Turn on the options that match how your business works, test each one by doing the action yourself and watching Realtime, and review the Events report monthly to see which interactions happen most and which pages they happen on, then decide which ones deserve to be marked as key events."),
"google-analytics-4-lead-generation-events": ("Connecting your CRM step by step", "If you use a CRM, the later lead stages can flow back to Analytics. The usual approach is to store the Analytics client identifier with each lead when the form is submitted, then send events such as qualify_lead or close_convert_lead from your CRM or server when the lead's status changes. Many CRMs offer integrations or support from their own help teams, and some marketing platforms do this automatically. Start with one stage, such as close_convert_lead when a job is booked, and check that the events appear in reports. Even a basic connection shows which channels bring paying customers rather than enquiries, which is the most useful number for deciding where an Anaheim or Orange County business should spend its marketing budget."),
}


def add_x(pages):
    for p in pages:
        if p["slug"] in X:
            h, b = X[p["slug"]]
            p["sections"].insert(len(p["sections"]) - 1, {"heading": h, "body_html": f"<p>{b}</p>"})
